/**
 * Completion-lane scoping-error detection (worker-completion-lane-evaluates-on-main,
 * task 2).
 *
 * The work-item completion lane scopes its Test Suite to the item's commit range
 * ("vitest --changed {sha}"). If the lane evaluates a tree that LACKS those
 * commits — a worker's commits live only on its unlanded run branch, but the lane
 * ran from main (the task-1 execution-context bug) — the changed test files are
 * absent on disk, so the scoped step runs NOTHING and exits non-zero ("No test
 * files found"). Recording that as an honest test failure is a lie: the work is
 * green in its worktree.
 *
 * Task 1 fixes the execution context so this should not happen; this is the loud
 * safety net. When the item's resolved range genuinely changed test files but the
 * scoped Test Suite step FAILED without running any test, {@link
 * evaluateCompletionStep} re-labels the outcome with a legible SCOPING reason
 * instead of letting the empty run masquerade as a genuine red.
 */
/**
 * The canonical analyser id of the vitest **Test Suite** step. The single source
 * of truth for "which completion step runs `vitest --changed`", shared by the
 * pipeline defaults (`pipeline-normalizer`) and the scoping-fault predicate below
 * so the two can never drift to different magic strings
 * (completion-lane-mutation-step-mislabelled-as-scoping-fault).
 */
export const TEST_ANALYZER_ID = 'test';
/**
 * Is this the one completion step that can suffer the wrong-tree scoping fault —
 * the vitest **Test Suite** step? ONLY `vitest --changed {sha}` fails by finding
 * no test files on disk (a non-zero exit with zero per-test failures). Other steps
 * that carry `runsTests: true` — Mutation Testing (`mutation-testing`), Code
 * Coverage (`coverage`) — fail on their OWN verdict (surviving mutants, a coverage
 * shortfall) and never produce that shape, so re-labelling them a scoping fault
 * launders a genuine red. This predicate is the discriminator the completion lane
 * gates on; keeping it here (pure and tested) is why the caller's decision is not
 * an untested magic-string comparison.
 */
export function stepSuffersScopingFault(step) {
    return step.analyzer === TEST_ANALYZER_ID;
}
const TEST_FILE_MARKERS = ['.test.', '.spec.', '__tests__', '.e2e.', '.integration.'];
/**
 * Does this path name a test file? The completion lane's test-bearing check —
 * the same markers the analyser uses to classify a changed file as a test.
 */
export function isTestFilePath(filePath) {
    return TEST_FILE_MARKERS.some((marker) => filePath.includes(marker));
}
/**
 * The persisted reason a scoping fault records — names the wrong-tree cause so a
 * reader (and the validation surfaces) never mistake it for a genuine red.
 */
export const COMPLETION_SCOPING_ERROR_REASON = "completion lane evaluated a tree without the item's commits: the item's range " +
    'changed test files, but the scoped Test Suite failed without running any test ' +
    '(a scoping fault — the changed tests are absent on disk — not a genuine red)';
/**
 * Decide the Test Suite step's final outcome. The caller invokes this ONLY for a
 * failed {@link stepSuffersScopingFault} step (the vitest Test Suite) — so no
 * discriminator is threaded here. When such a step FAILED against a test-bearing
 * range yet reported ZERO per-test failures — the shape of a run that found no
 * test files to execute (the wrong-tree scoping fault) — the outcome is re-labelled
 * a LOUD scoping error, preserving the step's label. Every other case passes the
 * outcome through unchanged: a green step, a genuine red with failing tests, and a
 * range that changed no test files.
 */
export function evaluateCompletionStep(input) {
    if (input.outcome.ok)
        return input.outcome;
    const changedTestFiles = input.rangeChangedFiles.filter(isTestFilePath);
    if (changedTestFiles.length > 0 && input.failingTestCount === 0) {
        return { ...input.outcome, ok: false, reasons: [COMPLETION_SCOPING_ERROR_REASON] };
    }
    return input.outcome;
}
